#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722480 :question/problem "![](images/22/22b88fbe643e04cad37a9e7c39f8862a73da413b5f78f8772bc5a3d18f048ed9.png)\n\nFind the exact arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=3 t + 1 \\\\ y&=2 t^{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ t&\\in\\left[1,2\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length is the integral of the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{(x\\prime(t))^2+(y\\prime(t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 t + 1\\right) \\\\ &=3 \\\\ y\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2&=\\left(3\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(3 \\sqrt{t}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=9 t + 9 \\\\ &=\\left(3 \\sqrt{t + 1}\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $3 \\sqrt{t + 1}\\geq0$, so taking the square root gives\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\sqrt{(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2}=3 \\sqrt{t + 1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the speed gives\n\n$$\n\\int 3 \\sqrt{t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=2 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at both endpoints and subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{2}3 \\sqrt{t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[2 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(6 \\sqrt{3}\\right)-\\left(4 \\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 \\sqrt{2} + 6 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722484 :question/problem "![](images/d5/d53de709940142c4c015fed29446843c18a2995f517d14bb581ef061bb7284d6.png)\n\nFind the exact arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{\\left(6 t + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} \\\\ y&=\\frac{9 t^{2}}{2} \\\\ t&\\in\\left[0,2\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length is the integral of the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(x\\prime(t))^2+(y\\prime(t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\left(6 t + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{6 t + 1} \\\\ y\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{9 t^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=9 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2&=\\left(3 \\sqrt{6 t + 1}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(9 t\\right)^2 \\\\ &=81 t^{2} + 54 t + 9 \\\\ &=\\left(9 t + 3\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $9 t + 3\\geq0$, so taking the square root gives\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\sqrt{(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2}=9 t + 3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the speed gives\n\n$$\n\\int 9 t + 3\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\frac{9 t^{2}}{2} + 3 t+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at both endpoints and subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2}9 t + 3\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{9 t^{2}}{2} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(24\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722490 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9e208d65e95d8e0b64c084e10f2f8f593b819e5f345cdefd292219758602406.png)\n\nFind the exact arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y&=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ t&\\in\\left[0,2 \\pi\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length is the integral of the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\sqrt{(x\\prime(t))^2+(y\\prime(t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates using the product rule; the opposite cross terms cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2&=\\left(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=4t^2(\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t) \\\\ &=4 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $2 t\\geq0$, so taking the square root gives\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\sqrt{(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2}=2 t.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the speed gives\n\n$$\n\\int 2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at both endpoints and subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\left(4 \\pi^{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=4 \\pi^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722499 :question/problem "![](images/37/370c211db91913c03ea76c81376dd33fab749723d1370db80222281e330d03cd.png)\n\nFind the exact arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=4 t - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y&=4 - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ t&\\in\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length is the integral of the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{(x\\prime(t))^2+(y\\prime(t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(4 t - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4 - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(4 - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2&=\\left(4 - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=32\\left(1-\\cos(t)\\right) \\\\ &=64\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $\\sin\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\geq0$, so taking the square root gives\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\sqrt{(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2}=8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{t}{2} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating, including the constant chain-rule factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\int 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{t}{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=- 16 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{t}{2} \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at both endpoints and subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{t}{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- 16 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{t}{2} \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- 8 \\sqrt{2}\\right)-\\left(-16\\right) \\\\ &=16 - 8 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722512 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t^{3} + 3\\\\\ny&=- 4 t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/6d/6dd336b159235693a0fb6748d1a40635b4a66423880dbc4ac894f466b887f396.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=3 t^{2}\\\\\ny'(t)&=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both derivatives and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=\\bigl(3 t^{2}\\bigr)^2+\\bigl(-4\\bigr)^2\\\\\n&=9 t^{4} + 16.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 16}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722514 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{3} + 1\\\\\ny&=- 3 t - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-2$ to $t=3$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/b9/b985592058f93d9f58609b6bd45e43996b9f422d12d0053c5eda1f15dc634a03.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=6 t^{2}\\\\\ny'(t)&=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both derivatives and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=\\bigl(6 t^{2}\\bigr)^2+\\bigl(-3\\bigr)^2\\\\\n&=36 t^{4} + 9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-2}^{3}\\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722527 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=- t^{2} - 1\\\\\ny&=- 3 t^{3} + 3 t - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=3$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/18/18fc45fce3c2cad760007c82f15b0103858b944fb2b30b939b31a33453dca748.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=- 2 t\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 9 t^{2} + 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both derivatives and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=\\bigl(- 2 t\\bigr)^2+\\bigl(- 9 t^{2} + 3\\bigr)^2\\\\\n&=81 t^{4} - 50 t^{2} + 9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{81 t^{4} - 50 t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722528 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{3} + 2 t^{2} - 3\\\\\ny&=2 t^{3} - 2 t^{2} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=3$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/10/10276f544790b5511e1067d1763f59699805a0364f1e1caccc434c9ed05d17bf.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=6 t^{2} + 4 t\\\\\ny'(t)&=6 t^{2} - 4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=72 t^{4} + 32 t^{2}\\\\\n&=(t)^2\\bigl(72 t^{2} + 32\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t)^2}=|t|$. The factor $t$ is nonnegative on this interval. Hence $|t|=t$ here.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(t\\right)\\sqrt{72 t^{2} + 32}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722532 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3 t^{3} + 2 t^{2} - 3\\\\\ny&=3 t^{3} - 2 t^{2} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/d3/d30f51b458dcd0e5f4b1b84e03ab791ebc56b1a73ae2da05677164f28468ae99.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=9 t^{2} + 4 t\\\\\ny'(t)&=9 t^{2} - 4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=162 t^{4} + 32 t^{2}\\\\\n&=(t)^2\\bigl(162 t^{2} + 32\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t)^2}=|t|$. The factor $t$ is nonnegative on this interval. Hence $|t|=t$ here.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(t\\right)\\sqrt{162 t^{2} + 32}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722543 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{3} + 3 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 3\\\\\ny&=2 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{3} - 3 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=3$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/78/78c5caf85c0c82de54ea5af8a6cfef3071538dec0af23d3dc7d46c841bccf51f.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=6 t + 6 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 12\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 6 t + 6 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{4} + 72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=(t - 2)^2\\bigl(72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 72\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t - 2)^2}=|t - 2|$. The factor $t - 2$ changes sign inside this interval, so its absolute value must remain.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left|t - 2\\right|\\sqrt{72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 72}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722545 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{3} + t^{2} - 1\\\\\ny&=2 t^{3} - t^{2} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-2$ to $t=3$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/bf/bf8d7feeb69c5e0093cf92b78cdb390a8aae5ee8b117ab637bafb120c4d2a06c.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=6 t^{2} + 2 t\\\\\ny'(t)&=6 t^{2} - 2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=72 t^{4} + 8 t^{2}\\\\\n&=(t)^2\\bigl(72 t^{2} + 8\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t)^2}=|t|$. The factor $t$ changes sign inside this interval, so its absolute value must remain.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-2}^{3}\\left|t\\right|\\sqrt{72 t^{2} + 8}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722547 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t^{3} + t^{2} + 3\\\\\ny&=t^{3} - t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/f7/f71d8f3c398126432ff79f87f94bdda0dbcbfd6c10ca7568eed1779eac8ff0f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=3 t^{2} + 2 t\\\\\ny'(t)&=3 t^{2} - 2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=18 t^{4} + 8 t^{2}\\\\\n&=(t)^2\\bigl(18 t^{2} + 8\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t)^2}=|t|$. The factor $t$ changes sign inside this interval, so its absolute value must remain.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left|t\\right|\\sqrt{18 t^{2} + 8}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722559 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{3} + 4 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} - 2\\\\\ny&=2 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{3} - 4 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=4$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/5c/5c51dabe9247857a3b15562c63525bcaeec213568c4c92f75f3740420201dfa4.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=8 t + 6 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} - 8\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 8 t + 6 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} + 8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=72 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{4} + 128 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=(t - 1)^2\\bigl(72 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} + 128\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t - 1)^2}=|t - 1|$. The factor $t - 1$ changes sign inside this interval, so its absolute value must remain.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{4}\\left|t - 1\\right|\\sqrt{72 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} + 128}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722570 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\left(t - 2\\right)^{3} + 4 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 1\\\\\ny&=\\left(t - 2\\right)^{3} - 4 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/92/92e4c0688c28ab5737615bf08d4be1badf95db8a399259875f9d259325a0e3a6.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=8 t + 3 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 16\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 8 t + 3 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 16.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=18 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{4} + 128 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=(t - 2)^2\\bigl(18 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 128\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t - 2)^2}=|t - 2|$. The factor $t - 2$ is nonpositive on this interval. Hence $|t - 2|=- t + 2$ here.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(- t + 2\\right)\\sqrt{18 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 128}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722583 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\left(t + 2\\right)^{3} + 2 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} + 1\\\\\ny&=\\left(t + 2\\right)^{3} - 2 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-5$ to $t=-2$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/d6/d64759d8e79e2b1434a612427d6ef89b30d7e48da3bc25c9c5a7d5092eb656b2.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=4 t + 3 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} + 8\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 4 t + 3 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} - 8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=18 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{4} + 32 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=(t + 2)^2\\bigl(18 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} + 32\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t + 2)^2}=|t + 2|$. The factor $t + 2$ is nonpositive on this interval. Hence $|t + 2|=- t - 2$ here.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-5}^{-2}\\left(- t - 2\\right)\\sqrt{18 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} + 32}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722584 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{3} + 4 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 1\\\\\ny&=2 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{3} - 4 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=5$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/cb/cb7864c48f7158f6d18786ff2ec469677c05d31cb59845d555cdb64ff0bea8f0.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=8 t + 6 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 16\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 8 t + 6 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 16.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{4} + 128 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=(t - 2)^2\\bigl(72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 128\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t - 2)^2}=|t - 2|$. The factor $t - 2$ is nonnegative on this interval. Hence $|t - 2|=t - 2$ here.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{2}^{5}\\left(t - 2\\right)\\sqrt{72 \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} + 128}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722586 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\left(t + 1\\right)^{3} + 3 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 3\\\\\ny&=\\left(t + 1\\right)^{3} - 3 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$?\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire interval. Do not evaluate it.\n\n![](images/bc/bcc1a1b8c0980d38e90a8988565875d87621d7b397f9a8b16bf25523df0dd991.png)" :question/worked-solution "For parametric arc length, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x\\prime(t)]^2+[y\\prime(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=6 t + 3 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 6\\\\\ny'(t)&=- 6 t + 3 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} - 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross terms cancel when the two derivative squares are added.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)^2+y'(t)^2&=18 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{4} + 72 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=(t + 1)^2\\bigl(18 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 72\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sqrt{(t + 1)^2}=|t + 1|$. The factor $t + 1$ is nonnegative on this interval. Hence $|t + 1|=t + 1$ here.\n\nUse the given parameter bounds:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(t + 1\\right)\\sqrt{18 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 72}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722599 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 1,\\qquad y=\\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$? Choose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. The two trig arguments differ, so the Pythagorean identity cannot combine these terms. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 64 \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722606 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 1,\\qquad y=t + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$? Choose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Expand the binomial square, including its signed cross term. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722608 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=7 t - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2,\\qquad y=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$? Choose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 7\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Expand the binomial square, then collect the squared sine terms. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 7\\right)^{2} + \\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{17 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 14 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 49}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722617 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1,\\qquad y=6 t + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$? Choose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=6 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Expand the binomial square, then collect the squared sine terms. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\left(6 - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 36}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722620 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=t - 1,\\qquad y=-1 + 2 e^{- t}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$? Choose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=1\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 2 e^{- t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(- 2 e^{- t}\\right)^{2} + 1^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + 4 e^{- 2 t}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722639 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=e^{t} + 1,\\qquad y=2 t + 2\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$? Use the arc-length formula.\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=e^{t}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\left(e^{t}\\right)^{2} + 2^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{e^{2 t} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722640 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=4 t - 1,\\qquad y=-1 + 5 e^{- t}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$? Use the arc-length formula.\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=4\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 5 e^{- t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\left(- 5 e^{- t}\\right)^{2} + 4^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16 + 25 e^{- 2 t}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722641 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=e^{t} + 2,\\qquad y=5 t + 1\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$? Use the arc-length formula.\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=e^{t}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(e^{t}\\right)^{2} + 5^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{e^{2 t} + 25}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722650 :question/problem "Which integral gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=1 + 4 e^{- t},\\qquad y=t + 1\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$? Use the arc-length formula.\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 4 e^{- t}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the complete derivatives before adding, and retain the parameter limits. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(- 4 e^{- t}\\right)^{2} + 1^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + 16 e^{- 2 t}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722663 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/aa/aa7d3041bfe9f1ccf260ecc694ffcd5084e8b00bd71482ecde611e383d4682cf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 4 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=\\frac{1}{t},\\ y=t$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}},1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(1,\\frac{1}{t^{2}})$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/67/670abe5e761fb3e80045bf6cf153979daa6ce099ecca125621749d794dcc534d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- 4 \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=- \\frac{4}{t} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\left(t\\right)^{2}=2 t^{2} \\\\\nx'&=- \\frac{1}{t^{2}} \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\nAt positive endpoints use $\\ln(1/u)=-\\ln u$, $\\ln u-\\ln v=\\ln(u/v)$, and $\\ln e=1$ as needed; retain the logarithm terms in the final value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}\\left[\\left(- \\frac{4}{t}\\right)(1)-(2 t^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(2 - \\frac{4}{t})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[2 t - 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\\n&=(4 - 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})-(1 + 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}) \\\\\n&=3 - 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722672 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/5b/5b30c80b806551a0945d42168bca59d027f13138d6d2b8ea7ae6338a4c4e8d28.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf i+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $1\\le t\\le e$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4 e^{- x})\\,\\mathbf i+(-1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\ y=2 t$, $1\\le t\\le e$. It runs from $(0,2)$ to $(1,2 e)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(\\frac{1}{t},2)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(2,- \\frac{1}{t})$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/e0/e09337a70ae1b90db8ae5b05c10b77ce4e6c57ccd071a9859ada26da55909891.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=\\frac{4}{t} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-1 \\\\\nx'&=\\frac{1}{t} \\\\\ny'&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\nAt positive endpoints use $\\ln(1/u)=-\\ln u$, $\\ln u-\\ln v=\\ln(u/v)$, and $\\ln e=1$ as needed; retain the logarithm terms in the final value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{1}^{e}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{e}\\left[\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)(2)-(-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{e}\\left(\\frac{9}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{1}^{e} \\\\\n&=(9)-(0) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722681 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/49/49f0859775561748963aaa618c05b0f7280c2180f8928a32067dd63b2b1fe8a5.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(2 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 3 y)\\,\\mathbf i+(3 x)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=2 t^{2},\\ y=t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(2,1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(4 t,1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(1,- 4 t)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/a9/a9ff8cd3eab429c5b4d983a20cc46d00213e77defa9b33f3021fc628b892b1e8.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- 3 \\left(t\\right)=- 3 t \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=3 \\left(2 t^{2}\\right)=6 t^{2} \\\\\nx'&=4 t \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(- 3 t)(1)-(6 t^{2})(4 t)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(- 24 t^{3} - 3 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[- 6 t^{4} - \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{15}{2}\\right)-(0) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722687 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/71/716409dd76b765e46f3c58757ae39d8a6d3b4f1979a19d1025a1b389248df9e2.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x y)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 4 x - 1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=t,\\ y=t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(1,1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(-1,1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/7a/7a994eb1ba591d8c8ea1ff4fb02eb8c183c9f566e9e2a7246854c4f94ae4e6ff.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- \\left(t\\right) \\left(t\\right)=- t^{2} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=- 4 \\left(t\\right) - 1=- 4 t - 1 \\\\\nx'&=1 \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(- t^{2})(1)-(- 4 t - 1)(1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(t^{2} - 4 t - 1)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^{3}}{3} - 2 t^{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right)-(0) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722692 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/ba/ba1497413ac0f51a0b3f74bacb325a5c73ddf3166cde4be09d1d5b7bcd010b7b.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(t)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4)\\,\\mathbf i+(2)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=t,\\ y=\\frac{1}{t}$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(1,- \\frac{1}{t^{2}})$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(\\frac{1}{t^{2}},1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/d7/d7ea4aed5cad1335458a52c27f75f98e93ab7c3e66ec85a809399e23465aead0.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\\\\nx'&=1 \\\\\ny'&=- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}\\left[(4)\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)-(2)(1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(2 + \\frac{4}{t^{2}})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[2 t - \\frac{4}{t}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\\n&=(2)-(-7) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722700 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line at $P=(1,0)$ to the curve $xe^{xy}+y^2=x^2+y$." :question/worked-solution "First express the curve as a level curve by moving all terms to the left:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=xe^{xy}+y^2-x^2-y=0.\n$$\n\nThe point is on the curve because $F(1,0)=1+0-1-0=0$. For the first term, use the product rule for the $x$ derivative and the chain rule for both partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_x(x,y)&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}-2x,\\\\ F_y(x,y)&=x^2e^{xy}+2y-1,\\\\ \\nabla F(1,0)&=\\langle 1+0-2,1+0-1\\rangle\\\\ &=\\langle -1,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero even though its second component is zero. It is normal to the tangent line, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\langle -1,0\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle&=0,\\\\ -(x-1)&=0,\\\\ x&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent is vertical. The zero value of $F_y(1,0)$ does not mean that the tangent is horizontal; the gradient points horizontally, and the tangent is perpendicular to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722704 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=x^2+xy+2y^2$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "For a nonzero gradient, the tangent line to a level curve at $P=(x_0,y_0)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(P)\\cdot\\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle=0.\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives and evaluate them at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2x+y,\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=x+4y,\\\\ \\nabla f(1,-1)&=\\langle 2(1)-1,1+4(-1)\\rangle\\\\ &=\\langle 1,-3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is normal to the level curve and to its tangent line. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\langle 1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y+1\\rangle&=0,\\\\ (x-1)-3(y+1)&=0,\\\\ x-3y-4&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $f(1,-1)=2$. The figure shows this level curve, its tangent, and a normal arrow in the gradient direction.\n\n![Level curve through P with its tangent and normal gradient arrow](images/e0/e0d4ac4ba1ada8c2611d8c6a2ea112f008a3891acb81a4312b851589453b131e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722717 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=e^{xy}+x^2-2y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "When taking a partial derivative, hold the other variable constant. The chain rule gives $\\partial(e^{xy})/\\partial x=ye^{xy}$ and $\\partial(e^{xy})/\\partial y=xe^{xy}$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=ye^{xy}+2x,\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=xe^{xy}-2,\\\\ \\nabla f(1,0)&=\\langle 0\\cdot e^0+2,1\\cdot e^0-2\\rangle\\\\ &=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero, so it is a normal vector to the tangent line. Using the displacement from $P$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,0)\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle&=0,\\\\ \\langle 2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle&=0,\\\\ 2(x-1)-y&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, the tangent is $2x-y-2=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722728 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/94/94dfc91c8d9e12a0edacfeebd60106fa15aa86b94e4cbf7fd058b827e23d1cd1.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 t)\\,\\mathbf i+(- t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4)\\,\\mathbf i+(-3)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- 2 t,\\ y=- t$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-4,-2)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-2,-1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(1,-2)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/ea/eaf9df468563558c596a3ef20c8150cab92f40a3f0db52cdb2ccf7ef0a85720d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-3 \\\\\nx'&=-2 \\\\\ny'&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[(4)(-1)-(-3)(-2)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}(10)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[10 t\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=(20)-(0) \\\\\n&=20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722729 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/ee/ee6fc7fe45aaf961e48cdee198b4e770c325f4231012ba6f71b060531273b44e.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- t)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(-1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- t,\\ y=2 t$, $0\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-2,4)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-1,2)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(-2,-1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/07/073fd5d0a886bf61e2fc8b9c4558aab79c8c2fe1d23aeae9d4102f538667501d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=-2 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-1 \\\\\nx'&=-1 \\\\\ny'&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[(-2)(2)-(-1)(-1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}(5)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[5 t\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=(10)-(0) \\\\\n&=10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722736 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/6a/6a90dacb53f070474d20a2f241bed17c9366d3bf2fe8875fbd6a4885ba5c7b17.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 t)\\,\\mathbf i+(- t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(-3)\\,\\mathbf i+(3)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- 2 t,\\ y=- t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-2,-1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-2,-1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(-1,2)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/f4/f4360dfd2574be37d49bfed630242fc0310008fc630461b8a3d79a87192722a7.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=-3 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=3 \\\\\nx'&=-2 \\\\\ny'&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(-3)(-1)-(3)(-2)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(9)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[9 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=(9)-(0) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722743 :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/7a/7a2389176221c3be048a9106e4f69d2154bf7ba36f6a6d986e062bc86ab61eb4.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 t)\\,\\mathbf i+(- t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2)\\,\\mathbf i+(-4)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only.\n\n*Hint:* Check the pictured normal against the tangent for increasing $t$. For a clockwise normal use $$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nFor an anticlockwise normal, negate this expression." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=- 2 t,\\ y=- t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$. It runs from $(0,0)$ to $(-2,-1)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(-2,-1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(1,-2)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/f0/f055ac9fb931827826dc450eefc37d947915ecbe54a18ef0f7037a50eee7d6b9.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-4 \\\\\nx'&=-2 \\\\\ny'&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{0}^{1}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[(2)(-1)-(-4)(-2)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}(10)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[10 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=(10)-(0) \\\\\n&=10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722747 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2+2y^2=3.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2.$ The point lies on the level curve $f(x,y)=3$ because $1^2+2(1)^2=3.$\n\nThe gradient is normal to the level curve. A nonzero vector perpendicular to it is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives, then evaluate at $(1,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x, & f_y(x,y)&=4y,\\\\\nf_x(1,1)&=2, & f_y(1,1)&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus a tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1)=4\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIndeed, $\\nabla f(1,1)=2\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf j$ is nonzero, and $(2)(4)+(4)(-2)=0.$ Any nonzero scalar multiple of $4\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j$ is also tangent; only one of the listed vectors has this direction.\n\nThe diagram shows the curve and the perpendicular normal and tangent directions at the point.\n\n![Ellipse with perpendicular normal and tangent directions at P](images/cc/cce5568a87e18aa17da2c3a8d86bcb81f735882a23cb8765df17cfa4e113c264.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722760 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2y=xy+y^2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Bring all variable terms to one side:\n\n$$\nx^2y-xy-y^2=0.\n$$\n\nSet $f(x,y)=x^2y-xy-y^2.$ Then the curve is $f(x,y)=0,$ and $f(2,2)=8-4-4=0.$\n\nA tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j.$ Differentiating while holding the other variable constant gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2xy-y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=x^2-x-2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the given point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,2)&=2(2)(2)-2=6,\\\\\nf_y(2,2)&=2^2-2-2(2)=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(2,2)=-2\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient $6\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j$ is nonzero, and $(6)(-2)+(-2)(-6)=0,$ so the vector is tangent. Any nonzero scalar multiple is also tangent; the other listed vectors are not perpendicular to this gradient."}]}
